<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<appendix xml:id="hks-notation">
  <title>Notation</title>
  <p><dl>
    <li><title><m>X,M,2n</m></title><p>An IHS manifold, its underlying smooth manifold, and its dimension. A general Calabi–Yau degeneration has dimension <m>d</m>; the hyperkähler application has <m>d=2n</m>. See <xref ref="hks-conventions" text="custom">the conventions</xref>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>q_X,c_X</m></title><p>The primitive integral BBF bilinear form and the Fujiki constant in <m>\int_X\alpha^{2n}=c_Xq_X(\alpha)^n</m>. We write <m>q_X(\alpha)=q_X(\alpha,\alpha)</m>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>L,\lambda,e</m></title><p>The specified nef isotropic line bundle, its first Chern class, and the positive primitive integral generator of its ray; <m>\lambda=m e</m> for an integer <m>m&gt;0</m>. See <xref ref="hks-nef-line-bundles"/>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>\nu(L),\kappa(L)</m></title><p>Numerical dimension and Iitaka dimension, defined in <xref ref="hks-def-semiample"/>. For nonzero nef isotropic <m>L</m>, <m>\nu(L)=n</m>; semiampleness gives <m>\kappa(L)=n</m>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>g,I,J,K;\ \omega_A</m></title><p>A hyperkähler metric, a quaternionic triple, and <m>\omega_A(u,v)=g(Au,v)</m>. Thus <m>IJ=K</m>, <m>\sigma_I=\omega_J+i\omega_K</m>, and <m>\sigma_J=\omega_K+i\omega_I</m>. See <xref ref="hks-cohomological-rotation"/>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>\Omega_J,\theta</m></title><p>The normalized volume form <m>\sigma_J^n/n!</m> and the phase of a special Lagrangian submanifold, measured by its positive calibration. See <xref ref="hks-def-special-lagrangian"/>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>F,F_{t,o},\operatorname{PD}[F]</m></title><p>A special Lagrangian or holomorphic Lagrangian torus, an angular coordinate torus near a maximal crossing, and its Poincaré dual with a specified orientation. See <xref ref="hks-def-profound-torus"/> and <xref ref="hks-profound-torus-class"/>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>T_k,N_k,W_\bullet</m></title><p>Monodromy on <m>H^k</m>, its logarithm after unipotent base change, and the limiting weight filtration centered at <m>k</m>. In degree two the prescribed degeneration has <m>\operatorname{im}N_2^2=\Q e</m>. See <xref ref="hks-degeneration-monodromy"/>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>h,P,e,f,v,a</m></title><p>The polarization class, a rational ternary space of signature <m>(2,1)</m>, and a basis with <m>q(e,f)=1</m>, <m>q(e)=q(f)=0</m>, <m>v\perp\langle e,f\rangle</m>, and <m>a=q(v)&gt;0</m>. These are local to the period construction in <xref ref="hks-prescribed-period"/>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>p(\tau),\tau=\alpha+iy</m></title><p>The normalized period <m>f+\tau v-a\tau^2e/2</m>. The real part <m>\alpha</m> is chosen irrational in the main proof; <m>y\to\infty</m> approaches the prescribed rational boundary point.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>s,\mu_t</m></title><p>The parameter <m>s=-\log|t|</m> and the probability measure obtained by normalizing <m>|\Omega_t|^2</m>. See <xref ref="hks-metric-degeneration"/>. A measure estimate uses this measure, whereas metric convergence requires a specified rescaling.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>Y_K^{\mathrm{an}},\operatorname{Sk}(\mathcal Y^*)</m></title><p>The analytification of the generic fiber over <m>K=\C((t))</m> and its essential skeleton. Valuative independence and weak comparison are defined in <xref ref="hks-def-valuative-independence"/> and <xref ref="hks-def-weak-comparison"/>.</p></li>
    <li><title><m>\mathcal T^\circ_e</m></title><p>The positive locus in a fixed marked Teichmüller component on which <m>e</m> is of type <m>(1,1)</m>. Its nef and semiample loci are compared in <xref ref="hks-thm-fixed-class-semiampleness"/>.</p></li>
  </dl></p>
</appendix>
